{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>To work the ground from which he was taken.</b> Not for nothing did Scripture mention 'from which he was taken.' I also found (in Midrash Shocher Tov, Psalm 92) that it is written that the first man settled on Mount Moriah — and from where did they learn to say so? It is also found among the Sages (Hullin 60a) that the first man offered a bull whose horn projected before its hoofs — from where did they have a scriptural basis for this? And it appears that the source for all these things is the verse 'from which he was taken,' that is, from the place concerning which it is said 'מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי' (Exodus 20:21). As the Sages said in Bereshit Rabbah (14:8): the Holy One, blessed be He, created him from the place of his atonement — would that it serve him for atonement! And that is Mount Moriah, to which HaShem sent him to work the ground and to build from it an altar of earth, and to offer upon it a sacrifice to atone for himself. And since he was taken from that earth, and it is the gateway through which he passed — for the earth imparted to him a thick and coarse materiality by reason of which he fell into sin, as explained above on the verse 'עֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי' (Genesis 1:11), see there — therefore in the place that caused the sin there shall be his atonement, for that place, that is, that earth, caused him to sin. Therefore that earth is obligated to assist him toward atonement, through his working it to make from it an altar upon which to offer a bull whose horn projects before its hoofs. And this is the correct reason for the commandment of 'מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי' — for with the very thing that caused the sin, one mends what one corrupted through it. And also according to the one who says He gathered his dust from all the earth — thus is its meaning: because the place concerning which it is said 'מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי,' there is the Foundation Stone, and from there the entire world was founded, and the dust that was taken from the center of the world is as though He gathered his dust from all the world. And through this is also settled what is said (Genesis 28:13), 'הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שֹׁכֵב עָלֶיהָ לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה.' Rashi explained: He folded the entire earth beneath him — not literally folded, but because he lay upon the navel of the earth and its center, therefore it is as though he lay upon the entire earth."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The citation 'חולין ס.' refers to Hullin 60a; the abbreviation 'רז״ל' resolved as חז״ל based on parallel sources; the phrase 'פר מקרין מפריס' denotes a bull whose horn grows before its hoofs, a rabbinic description of Adam's offering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:59.882376645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}